EXT. FLOODED SUGAR MILL - DUSK

A war-wrecked sugar mill on a river plain. The ground floor stands in knee-deep brown water. Chimney broken. Rain has thinned to drizzle.

Three soldiers wade between iron vats, rifles above their heads. All three are exhausted and trying not to show it.

CAPTAIN RUTH OYELOWO-KAUFMANN (40s, steady, carries a map case) goes first, testing each step with a pole. SERGEANT BASHIR AL-HAMDANI (30s, broad shoulders, a limp he hides) follows, watching the ridge line. A young MEDIC (20s, red cross armband, boots gone) brings up the rear, carrying a pack too big for him.

CAPTAIN
Stairs on the left. We take the second floor and dry out.

SERGEANT
Dry out, Captain? With what?

CAPTAIN
With hope, Sergeant.

The Medic stumbles on a submerged rail. The Sergeant catches him by the strap and sets him upright without a word, and the Captain stops, turns, and watches the boy catch his breath before she moves on.

INT. SUGAR MILL - SECOND FLOOR - NIGHT

A long room of rotted grain sacks and a cold boiler. A hole in the roof lets in the rain. The three of them drop their packs.

The Captain spreads her map on a barrel and studies it with a pencil stub. The Sergeant props his rifle at the window, rubs his knee, then looks out at the dark plain. The Medic sits against the wall, unlaces his sodden boots, and winces as he peels them off.

SERGEANT
How far to the bridge?

CAPTAIN
Fourteen kilometers.

SERGEANT
In the morning?

CAPTAIN
At dawn. If the river lets us.

MEDIC
(small voice)
The river's already let us. It came up to meet us.

The Sergeant almost smiles. The Captain doesn't look up from her map.

CAPTAIN
You'll want those feet dry, Medic.

MEDIC
Yes, ma'am.

CAPTAIN
Then dry them. That's an order.

The Medic wrings out a sock. The Sergeant sits beside him and hands over a rag from his own kit.

SERGEANT
Use this. It's clean. Mostly.

MEDIC
Thank you.

SERGEANT
What's your name, son? I've been calling you Medic for six days.

MEDIC
Ibrahim, Sergeant. Ibrahim Osei.

SERGEANT
Ibrahim. Good. A man needs a name in the dark.

The Captain finally looks up. A beat. Something eases in her face.

CAPTAIN
Ibrahim, how old are you?

IBRAHIM
Nineteen, ma'am.

CAPTAIN
Nineteen.

She says it softly, as if turning the word over to find out what it weighs. Then she folds the map, takes a tin from her pocket, and pries it open.

CAPTAIN (CONT'D)
Rations. One tin of peaches. We split it three ways.

SERGEANT
Peaches? Where did you get peaches?

CAPTAIN
I've carried them since the coast. I was saving them.

SERGEANT
For what?

CAPTAIN
For a night like this one. When nothing else was left to save them for.

She passes the tin. The Sergeant takes a slice and passes it on. Ibrahim takes a slice and holds it up to the dim light like a jewel before he eats it.

IBRAHIM
It's sweet.

SERGEANT
It is.

CAPTAIN
Eat the syrup too. We'll want the sugar tomorrow.

They drink the syrup in turns, silently. Rain ticks on the sacks. Below them the water slaps against the vats.

The Sergeant takes the first watch at the window. The Captain lies down with her pack for a pillow, her eyes open. Ibrahim curls on his side, hands tucked between his knees.

IBRAHIM
(half-asleep)
Sergeant? Do you think we'll make the bridge?

SERGEANT
(not turning)
Sleep, Ibrahim.

IBRAHIM
But do you think...

SERGEANT
I think the bridge has waited this long. It can wait for you.

Ibrahim's breath slows. The Captain closes her eyes. The Sergeant stays at the window, a shape against the gray, listening to the rain and the river and, beyond them, nothing at all.
